1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,972
A, B and D represent a target stand; C a comparison stand.

2
00:00:05,434 --> 00:00:08,246
Which trees change around 2000?.

3
00:00:08,553 --> 00:00:12,750
Synchrony is evidence to investigate, not proof of a cause.

4
00:00:13,211 --> 00:00:17,586
This synthetic graph contrasts a target stand with a comparison.

5
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:23,280
Inspect similarities and differences in changes around the year two thousand.

6
00:00:24,083 --> 00:00:31,683
M1: mean for 1995–1999; M2: mean for 2000–2004.

7
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:38,552
Percent change G = (M2 − M1) / M1 times 100%.

8
00:00:39,006 --> 00:00:43,756
Five-year windows are a teaching choice, not a universal criterion.

9
00:00:44,217 --> 00:00:47,618
Changing the averaging windows can change the result.

10
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:53,500
The five-year windows here are a teaching choice, not a universal procedure.

11
00:00:54,303 --> 00:00:57,436
Compute percent changes from the period means.

12
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:02,141
Compare A and D using percentages and absolute changes.

13
00:01:02,595 --> 00:01:04,384
Can you infer a gap?

14
00:01:04,845 --> 00:01:06,989
Suggest further evidence.

15
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:13,121
Pause to calculate percentage and absolute change, paying attention to percentages computed

16
00:01:13,134 --> 00:01:15,704
from small initial values.

17
00:01:16,507 --> 00:01:22,691
A and D both increase 100%, but by 1.0 and 0.1 mm.

18
00:01:22,809 --> 00:01:26,534
B increases 50%; C is unchanged.

19
00:01:26,996 --> 00:01:28,845
Compare the tree groups.

20
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:33,608
The calculation alone does not establish a cause or event date.

21
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:38,568
Equal percentage increases can represent very different added widths.

22
00:01:39,022 --> 00:01:43,260
Compare the starting level and absolute increment as well.

23
00:01:44,063 --> 00:01:48,084
Small prior growth can produce large percentage changes.

24
00:01:48,539 --> 00:01:52,572
Choose criteria appropriate to species and growth conditions.

25
00:01:53,026 --> 00:01:57,664
Do not turn 100% into a universal disturbance threshold.

26
00:01:58,118 --> 00:02:03,276
Evaluate prior growth and species differences rather than treating one numerical threshold

27
00:02:03,289 --> 00:02:05,920
as applicable to every tree.

28
00:02:06,723 --> 00:02:10,875
BAI measures the increase in stem cross-sectional area.

29
00:02:11,331 --> 00:02:17,844
For a circular stem: BAI = pi [(r + w) squared − r squared].

30
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:23,331
Equal widths can add different areas; BAI alone is not biomass.

31
00:02:23,791 --> 00:02:27,429
Basal area increment is a geometric stem measure.

32
00:02:27,883 --> 00:02:33,260
Converting it directly to carbon would ignore volume and wood-density information.

33
00:02:34,063 --> 00:02:38,011
Establishment differs from the first ring at sampling height.

34
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:42,236
Consider missing pith and years needed to reach that height.

35
00:02:42,697 --> 00:02:47,026
Present-day age structure does not recover every past individual.

36
00:02:47,486 --> 00:02:52,697
Distinguish the first preserved ring at sampling height from germination or establishment,

37
00:02:52,855 --> 00:02:55,224
and consider missing pith.

38
00:02:56,027 --> 00:02:59,495
Death, logging and decay remove older evidence.

39
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:04,713
Consider dead wood, stumps, regeneration and forest records.

40
00:03:05,174 --> 00:03:09,220
Report sampling criteria and the number of trees through time.

41
00:03:09,674 --> 00:03:12,950
Surviving trees may differ from those that died.

42
00:03:13,411 --> 00:03:17,716
Consider the individuals absent from the available record.
